.Dd $Mdocdate: August 2 2026 $
.Dt FuguLib::EventLoop 3p
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm FuguLib::EventLoop
.Nd one select loop for a daemon with one process
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Bd -literal
use FuguLib::EventLoop;

my $loop = FuguLib::EventLoop->new(signal => $sig);

$loop->add_fd($listener, read => sub ($fh) { accept_one($fh) });
$loop->every(1, sub { $mqtt->tick(0) });
$loop->every(30, sub { $mqtt->reconnect unless $mqtt->is_connected });

my $flush = $loop->after(0.25, sub { flush_events() });

$loop->run;	# returns when a signal or a callback stops it
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the loop that a daemon with one process and no threads runs.
A caller registers descriptors and timers, then calls
.Fn run .
.Pp
Written by hand, that loop becomes one
.Ic while
with a poll interval chosen for whichever job needs it most, an epoch
comparison for each periodic job, and no way out but
.Xr exit 3
inside a signal handler.
This module is that loop, written one time.
.Pp
The select timeout comes from the nearest timer deadline.
Thus a loop whose only job runs every second does not wake ten times
a second, and a loop with a 250 ms job does not miss it by a whole
interval.
.Pp
There are no threads.
A callback runs in the process that called
.Fn run ,
between passes, and nothing else runs while it does.
A callback that blocks blocks the loop, so keep the work short or
queue it.
.Ss new
.Fn new "%args"
creates a loop.
.Pp
These are the arguments:
.Bl -tag -width "signalXX"
.It Fa log
The logger for a callback that dies.
The default is the process default of
.Xr FuguLib::Log 3p .
.It Fa signal
A
.Xr FuguLib::Signal 3p
whose interrupt flag ends the loop.
.El
.Ss add_fd
.Fn add_fd "$fh" "%args"
watches a descriptor.
The loop calls the
.Fa read
callback with the handle when the handle is readable.
.Pp
A second
.Fn add_fd
on the same handle replaces the callback rather than adding one, so a
caller that re-registers does not get two calls for one readable
event.
.Pp
The callback must read with
.Xr sysread 2 ,
not with a buffered read.
A buffered read can take more bytes than the callback consumes, and
then the descriptor is not readable again and the rest is never
served.
.Ss remove_fd
.Fn remove_fd "$fh"
stops watching a descriptor.
The loop does not close it: the owner of a handle closes it.
.Ss has_fd
.Fn has_fd "$fh"
reports if the loop watches a descriptor.
.Ss every
.Fn every "$seconds" "$code"
runs the callback every
.Fa $seconds .
The method returns a handle for
.Fn cancel .
.Ss after
.Fn after "$seconds" "$code"
runs the callback one time,
.Fa $seconds
from now.
The method returns a handle for
.Fn cancel .
.Pp
A caller that schedules the same work again before it runs gets two
runs.
Keep the handle, or test it first.
.Ss cancel
.Fn cancel "$handle"
drops a timer.
Cancelling a timer that already ran, or one that was cancelled, does
nothing.
.Ss stop
.Fn stop
ends the loop after the current pass.
A callback calls this to stop the loop it runs inside.
.Ss is_running
.Fn is_running
reports if the loop is between the start and the end of
.Fn run .
.Ss signal
.Fn signal "$manager"
sets or reads the signal manager.
A caller that builds the loop before the manager uses this instead of
the constructor argument.
.Ss run
.Fn run
watches and dispatches until something stops the loop.
.Pp
Three things stop it: a callback that calls
.Fn stop ,
an interrupt flag on the signal manager, and a loop with no
descriptor and no timer left, which has nothing to wait for.
.Pp
The last one matters to a caller that drives the loop for one piece
of work: register the work, call
.Fn run ,
and the method returns when the work is done.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn every
and
.Fn after
return a timer handle.
.Fn cancel
returns 1 when it removed a timer and 0 when there was none.
.Fn has_fd
and
.Fn is_running
return 1 or 0.
Every other method returns the object.
.Sh EXAMPLES
A daemon that ends on a signal, and shuts down where an operator can
read it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
my $sig = FuguLib::Signal->new;
$sig->setup_interrupt_flag('INT', 'TERM');

my $loop = FuguLib::EventLoop->new(signal => $sig);
$loop->add_fd($listener, read => \&accept_one);
$loop->run;

$log->info('Shutting down');
withdraw_advertisement();
.Ed
.Sh ERRORS
.Fn add_fd
dies without a code reference, and with a handle that has no
descriptor.
A timer dies without a code reference and without a positive
interval.
Those are programming errors.
.Pp
A callback that dies does not stop the loop.
The loop logs the reason and carries on.
A daemon that exits because one connection misbehaved is a daemon
that a peer can stop.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr FuguLib::Log 3p ,
.Xr FuguLib::Signal 3p ,
.Xr IO::Select 3p
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Dick Olsson Aq Mt hi@senzilla.io
.Sh CAVEATS
A pass never sleeps longer than one second, even with no timer due.
The loop must come back often enough to see a stop request, and a
caller must not have to add a timer of its own to get that.
.Pp
A periodic timer measures its next deadline from the end of the
callback, not from the old deadline.
A callback that overran does not then run several times in a row to
catch up.
Thus the interval is a minimum gap, not a rate.
.Pp
The loop watches for readable only.
A caller that must know when a descriptor is writable, or that writes
more than the socket buffer holds, needs a queue of its own.
